  "forwardSecrecyTitle": "Opened once, on this device",
  "@forwardSecrecyTitle": {},
  "forwardSecrecyNotice": "Each locked message can be opened once, on this device only. Your history stays in the app — export an archive to back it up. A new device cannot read old messages.",
  "@forwardSecrecyNotice": {},
  "aboutEncryptionTitle": "About encryption",
  "@aboutEncryptionTitle": {},
  "aboutEncryptionHistoryTitle": "Your history on this device",
  "@aboutEncryptionHistoryTitle": {},
  "aboutEncryptionBodyHistory": "Every text you lock or open is kept in the app, sealed per person under a key only this device holds. Your master password protects every key: without it, or your recovery key, nothing kept here can be opened. My safe opens with the master password or with a password of its own; giving it its own password also gives it a new key and a new recovery key. Files are kept the same way: the app keeps only the sealed file and unlocks it for the moment you open, save or share it. A file you save or share is yours — it leaves the app as an ordinary file, so protect it like any other file. The app locks itself after the idle time you choose, and when it has been in the background for a while. If you turn on fingerprint or face unlock, your password is stored on this device so your fingerprint or face can release it.",
  "@aboutEncryptionBodyHistory": {},
  "aboutEncryptionWindowTitle": "Open in order",
  "@aboutEncryptionWindowTitle": {},
  "aboutEncryptionBodyWindow": "The app keeps keys for skipped messages, but not without limit. A message more than 63 behind the newest one you already opened from that person can no longer be opened — the app says it is too old — and no more than 40 skipped messages are kept at once. You cannot open your own sent messages either; that is why the app keeps what you sent.",
  "@aboutEncryptionBodyWindow": {},
  "aboutEncryptionLinksTitle": "Message links",
  "@aboutEncryptionLinksTitle": {},
  "aboutEncryptionBodyLinks": "A link carries only the locked message, after the # — the part a browser never sends to any server. A link never opens anything by itself: the app shows who it is from and opens it only when you tap Open. A reply link connects you only when you tap Connect.",
  "@aboutEncryptionBodyLinks": {},
  "aboutEncryptionErrorsTitle": "What the errors mean",
  "@aboutEncryptionErrorsTitle": {},
  "aboutEncryptionBodyErrors": "“Already opened”: this message was opened on this device before, or its one-time key is gone — more than 40 messages before it were skipped, or the conversation has moved on several rounds since — so it can never be read here. “Not a valid locked message”: it is damaged, incomplete, or belongs to a different person — the app cannot tell these apart. “Too old”: it is more than 63 messages behind the newest one you already opened from that person. “Incorrect key” / “Wrong password” (Basics): the key or password does not match, or the text or file is damaged — the app cannot tell these apart either. For files, a receive that fails or is cancelled after it started has already used the file’s one-time key: ask the sender to send the file again.",
  "@aboutEncryptionBodyErrors": {},
  "aboutEncryptionBodySafetyNumber": "Each person you connect with has a 60-digit safety number, computed from both devices’ identity keys. Compare it with them by voice or in person: if the digits match, nobody sat in the middle when you connected. “Checked” is your own note that you did this — the app cannot check it for you.",
  "@aboutEncryptionBodySafetyNumber": {},
  "aboutEncryptionUnderTheHoodTitle": "Under the hood",
  "@aboutEncryptionUnderTheHoodTitle": {},
  "aboutEncryptionBodyUnderTheHood": "Messages use the Olm double ratchet (vodozemac, independently audited) — a fresh key per message, which is what makes a locked message single-use. Files are sealed with XChaCha20-Poly1305 in chunks; a damaged file is refused and nothing is left on disk. Passwords go through Argon2id. Your secret keys live in the app’s native core and are never sent anywhere. There is no web version: the core is native code.",
  "@aboutEncryptionBodyUnderTheHood": {},
